/* i18n-todo kb.najia */
"kb.najia.body" = "## Eight Palaces, Self and Other\nSix-line najia divination goes back to Jing Fang of the Western Han, and later came to be called the Huozhulin method. Jing Fang's Commentary on the Changes (Jingshi Yizhuan) divides the sixty-four hexagrams among eight palaces: Heaven, Thunder, Water, Mountain, Earth, Wind, Fire and Lake. Each palace is headed by its doubled trigram, the palace head. Changing its lines one at a time from the bottom gives the first- through fifth-generation hexagrams; changing the fourth line of the fifth-generation hexagram gives the wandering soul; restoring the inner trigram of the wandering soul to the palace trigram gives the returning soul. Every hexagram has a self line (shi) standing for the one who asks: the top line in the palace head, the line of the same number in the first- to fifth-generation hexagrams, the fourth line in a wandering soul and the third in a returning soul. The line three places from the self is the other (ying), standing for the other party or the matter asked about. A hexagram takes the phase of its palace: Coming to Meet (Gou), for example, belongs to the Heaven palace and is Metal.\n\n## Stems, Branches and the Six Relations\nNajia, literally “receiving the stems,” assigns a Heavenly Stem and an Earthly Branch to each line. Heaven takes Jia in the inner trigram and Ren in the outer, Earth takes Yi inside and Gui outside, Thunder takes Geng, Wind Xin, Water Wu, Fire Ji, Mountain Bing and Lake Ding. Branches run forward for yang trigrams and backward for yin ones; Heaven, from the bottom up, reads Zi, Yin, Chen, Wu, Shen, Xu. Each branch has a phase, and taking the palace phase as the self gives the six relations: the same phase is Siblings, what the self generates is Offspring, what it restrains is Wealth, what restrains it is Officials, and what generates it is Parents. Jing Fang's text already holds the germ of this scheme; the names were fixed later.\n\n## Six Spirits, Void Branches, Hidden Spirits\nThe six spirits are laid from the first line upward according to the day's stem: Jia and Yi days start with the Azure Dragon, Bing and Ding with the Vermilion Bird, Wu with the Gouchen, Ji with the Soaring Serpent (Tengshe), Geng and Xin with the White Tiger, Ren and Gui with the Black Tortoise, cycling in that order. Void branches follow the ten-day period of the day pillar: ten stems meet twelve branches, so each period leaves two branches empty; in the Jia-Zi period, Xu and Hai are void. If one of the six relations is missing from the hexagram, it is sought in the palace head; the line found there is the hidden spirit, lying beneath the flying spirit at the same position.\n\n## Month and Day\nReading the lines also involves the month branch and the day. The month changes at the solar terms, so the Yin month begins only at the Beginning of Spring; the day is that day's stem and branch. A line supported by the month or day is strong, one restrained by them weak, and these are then weighed together with changing lines and void branches. Qing manuals such as Additions and Deletions to Divination (Zengshan Buyi) and The True Lineage of Divination (Bushi Zhengzong) discuss this at length, and schools differ in the details.\n\n## How This App Does It\nThis app only lays out the chart: palace, self and other, stems and branches, the six relations, the six spirits, hidden spirits and changed lines, with the stems and branches of the year, month, day and hour and the void branches. It does not pick a focal line, judge strength or reach a conclusion. The layout is objective; the reading belongs to you, so treat the chart as a map for careful looking.";
